Chapter: LensSpec
  cites Foreword chapter Console
  cites Foreword chapter ListUtils
  cites Safari chapter Lens
  cites Judge chapter Grade

 Lens: the base focal and the two narrowings that pull it in.

 Nothing in judge/ grades this chapter. Camera has a check and Lens was split
 out of it on 2026-09-03, taking the focal and the pull-ins with it and leaving
 the check behind on the projection -- so the split moved code out from under
 its own oracle, which is a thing splits do quietly and this file is the repair.

 WHERE THE EXPECTED VALUES COME FROM: the definitions, in arithmetic that owes
 the chapter nothing. The base focal is a half-width over the tangent of half the
 field of view -- 480 / tan 35 degrees -- and the two narrowings are that number
 times the factor each formula gives. None of it was captured. Writing the wants
 as `focal * 0.35` would have been a tautology dressed as a test: it would pass
 against any base focal at all, including a wrong one.

 THE TOLERANCE IS MEASURED AND THE MEASUREMENT IS THE INTERESTING PART. `r-tan`
 is `real-sin x / real-cos x` and DeviceMath's sine is a truncated Taylor series,
 so the focal inherits that error: graded relatively, this file FAILS at 1e-10
 and PASSES at 1.2e-10, which puts the error just over 1e-10 relative -- about
 7e-8 of a pixel on a 685-pixel focal. That is three hundred times smaller than
 the 5.6e-8 absolute error TrigSpec measures at the same series' worst argument,
 because 35 degrees is nowhere near the quadrant fold's worst point. The gate is
 1e-9, the next round decade above what was measured.

 THE WANTS BEING TRUE VALUES IS WHAT MAKES THAT GATE SAFE IN THE RIGHT
 DIRECTION. Upstream issue 122 asks for two more terms in the sine; if it lands,
 these gots move TOWARD the wants rather than away, because the wants are the
 mathematics and the error is the implementation's. A file whose wants had been
 captured from today's output would break on that fix and read as a regression.

 We say:
Section: The Base Focal

 480 pixels of half-width over tan 35 degrees. Computed from the definition, not
 captured: the chapter says the base focal is a half-width over the tangent of
 half the field of view, and 960 / 2 over tan(70 / 2) is 685.5110432362151.

  base-got : List Real
  base-got = [camera-w, fov-deg, focal]

  base-want : List Real
  base-want = [960.0, 70.0, 685.5110432362151]

Section: The Two Narrowings

 The lean narrows as the SQUARE of the fraction, down to 0.35 of the base at
 full lean; attention narrows LINEARLY, down to 0.61 at full gaze. Both wants
 are the base focal times the factor the chapter's own formula gives, worked out
 independently rather than by calling the chapter.

 ATTENTION IS GRADED PAST 1.0 ON PURPOSE. The chapter says a crossing cat's
 attention overshoots one, so 1.5 is a value the game produces and the formula
 has no clamp: it gives 0.415 of the base, which pulls in HARDER than a full
 gaze at 0.61 and harder than a full lean at 0.35 would if squared. A spec that
 stopped at 1.0 would leave the only regime where gaze beats lean untested.

  lean-in : List Real
  lean-in = [0.0, 0.25, 0.5, 0.75, 1.0]

  lean-want : List Real
  lean-want = [685.5110432362151, 657.6621571047439, 574.1154987103301, 434.8710680529739, 239.92886513267527]

  gaze-in : List Real
  gaze-in = [0.0, 0.5, 1.0, 1.5]

  gaze-want : List Real
  gaze-want = [685.5110432362151, 551.8363898051531, 418.16173637409116, 284.4870829430293]

Section: The Tighter One Wins

 Six pairs, chosen so that each narrowing wins some of them and the two are
 EQUAL in one. At (0, 0) neither narrows and the two are the same number, which
 is the case that would let a `min` written backwards still pass every other
 line here -- so the pairs after it split three to two the other way.

  cam-lean : List Real
  cam-lean = [0.0, 1.0, 0.0, 0.5, 0.9, 0.6]

  cam-gaze : List Real
  cam-gaze = [0.0, 0.0, 1.0, 0.3, 1.4, 0.5]

  cam-want : List Real
  cam-want = [685.5110432362151, 239.92886513267527, 418.16173637409116, 574.1154987103301, 311.2220136292417, 525.1014591189407]

  cam-walk : Integer -> List Real
  cam-walk (i) =
    if i >= list-length cam-lean then []
    else [cam-focal (list-at cam-lean i) (list-at cam-gaze i)] & cam-walk (i + 1)

Section: Entry

  opening : [Console] Nothing = act
    print-line-uni (grade-rel "l-base " base-got base-want 0.000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel "l-lean " (list-map focal-for-lean lean-in) lean-want 0.000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel "l-gaze " (list-map focal-for-gaze gaze-in) gaze-want 0.000000001)
    print-line-uni (grade-rel "l-cam  " (cam-walk 0) cam-want 0.000000001)
  end
